<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Developer ID / notarized build (app/build-dmg.sh --developer-id).

  Only the hardened runtime needs these; the ad-hoc build uses none of them. Both keys were
  written when the engine was a Rust dylib loaded across the FFI boundary. That is no longer
  the build: `crates/qh-ffi` also produces a `staticlib`, and the app links the archive, so
  the engine's code is inside the app binary and nothing is loaded from outside the bundle.

  Which means disable-library-validation may now be unnecessary. It is kept until that is
  measured rather than argued: proving it means launching a hardened-runtime bundle without
  the key, and this machine has no Developer ID certificate to sign one with. Dropping a key
  on reasoning alone risks an app that crashes for the users who signed correctly. ADR-0014
  records the same conclusion.

  NOT YET VERIFIED against a real Developer ID: this machine has no such certificate, so the
  first notarized build must be launched and its engine exercised (Test Connection, then an
  export) before it is published.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Suspected dead weight since the engine became static; see the note above and ADR-0014.
       If it is still needed, the reason is narrower than it used to be. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Kept for the FFI call path rather than for the engine's own code: the Swift/libffi
       marshalling layer around a call into Rust allocates executable pages, which the
       hardened runtime refuses by default. The Rust crates themselves forbid `unsafe_code`
       and need no writable-executable memory of their own. A static engine does not change
       this: it is the same trampoline making the same call. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
